.TH STYXDIRTEST 1
.SH NAME
styxdirtest \- check that a 9P directory's "do not change" sentinels survive a decode
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B styxdirtest
.SH DESCRIPTION
A 9P
.B wstat
field of
.B "all ones"
means
.I "do not change this field" .
.IR styx (2)'s
.B unpackdir
builds each one into a Limbo
.BR int ,
which is 64 bits at runtime, so an all-ones 32-bit field decodes to
.B 4294967295
and not
.BR ~0 .
Every consumer written as
.EX
	if (d.mtime != ~0)
		onto.mtime = d.mtime;
.EE
then takes the sentinel for a real value and writes it.
.B 0xFFFFFFFF
seconds from 1970 is
.BR 2106\-02\-07 ,
which is where files stamped 2106 come from.
.PP
.I Styxdirtest
packs
.B Sys->nulldir
\(em whose every scalar field is the sentinel \(em reads it back through
.BR unpackdir ,
and requires each field to come out as
.BR ~0 .
It then packs a dir carrying a plausible real time and requires that to
round-trip unchanged, which is the arm that stops a fix being worse than
the bug.
.PP
The widths differ and so do the literals:
.B mode ,
.BR atime ,
.BR mtime ,
.B dev
and
.B qid.vers
are four bytes,
.B dtype
is two and
.B qid.qtype
one, so their sentinels are
.BR 16rFFFFFFFF ,
.B 16rFFFF
and
.B 16rFF
respectively.
.B length
and
.B qid.path
are eight and were always correct, because they are built into a
.BR big .
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/styxdirtest.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR styx (2)
.SH BUGS
.B "It tests the boundary, not the consumers."
Eight files in this tree compare a dir field against
.B ~0
\(em
.BR nametree.b ,
.BR memfs.b ,
.BR install/eproto.b ,
.BR install/proto.b ,
.BR disk/mkfs.b ,
.BR fs/chstat.b ,
.BR fs/proto.b
and
.B alphabet/fs/chstat.b
\(em and none of them is exercised here. A consumer that compared
against the wrong value would still pass this test.
.PP
.B "It does not cover the C side."
.B Sys_Dir
in
.B libinterp/runt.h
holds these fields as
.BR WORD ,
which is
.B intptr
and so 64 bits, and
.B emu/port/inferno.c
converts in both directions. The Limbo-to-C direction truncates
correctly and the C-to-Limbo direction zero-extends, but neither is
tested here, and a sentinel arriving from a
.IR stat (2)
rather than off the wire takes that path instead.
.PP
.B "Only the sentinel and one real time are tried."
Nothing checks a value one below the sentinel, a post-2038 time, or a
field whose real value happens to be all ones \(em which 9P reserves, so
it cannot occur, but nothing here demonstrates that.
.PP
Printing is in
.B big
throughout, deliberately.
.B %d
and
.B %x
both format the low 32 bits, so
.B 4294967295
and
.B ~0
both print as
.BR \-1 :
two earlier versions of this test reported
.B "got -1, want -1"
on a
.I failing
line, which reads as a broken test rather than a real inequality. If a
future field is added here, widen it before printing.
